# a3_threads16
#
# donor      SHARK thread sweep and BumbleBee x8_omp1, both measured primaries, both no-ops:
#              SHARK      T4/T8/T16, peak < 0.3% at all but one step; glibc arenas vs threads +0.03%
#                         over 4x (shark/off-path.md)
#              BumbleBee  x8_omp1: 15 of 16 OMP threads removed, peak within noise, wrong direction,
#                         runtime unchanged (bumblebee/off-path-runs/x8_omp1/RESULT.md)
# mechanism  fewer worker threads: per-worker state held fewer times; costs runtime only.
#            ARION_THREADS is a declared parameter (upstream 64).
#
# pool_attacked: host
# object_attacked: rlwe.NewElement
#
# corrected  at harvest 2026-08-13 (was go:runtime_retained, rejected by the build). Measured differences:
#              112.39 ->  95.73 GiB   -16.66   rlwe.NewElement            <- the object, and the largest drop
#               39.23 ->  29.98 GiB    -9.25   ringqp.Ring.NewPoly
#               11.53 ->   8.38 GiB    -3.15   rlwe.(*Element).CopyNew
#               62.34 ->  59.45 GiB    -2.88   go:runtime_retained        <- what the old declaration named
#               12.07 ->   9.51 GiB    -2.56   anon:residual
# cost_class: paid
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# baseline   64 pinned (off-path.md: thread count is a legitimate step, spent here openly).
# no rebuild ARION_THREADS from the environment (impl/pin_workload.py); same image as a1, a2;
#            RUN_META arion_threads=16.
# object     a2 rank 1 rlwe.NewElement (46.45%), callers:
#              ckks.Evaluator.AddNew                         45,088.8 MB
#              polynomial ... EvaluateBabyStep               34,232.9 MB
#              dft.(*Evaluator).SlotsToCoeffsNew             14,492.9 MB
#              ckks.Evaluator.MulRelinNew                    12,410.9 MB
# borrow     BumbleBee b1_dot_encode_chunk does not apply: the loop is already chunked to one
#            ciphertext per worker:
#                ApproximatePolynomialChebyshevMT (pkg/math/activation/polynomialMT.go:27)
#                    chunkSize := (numCts + numThreads - 1) / numThreads
#                    for t := 0; t < numThreads; t++ { go func(start, end int) {
#                        localEval := eval.ShallowCopy(); polyEval := polynomial.NewEvaluator(...)
#                        for i := start; i < end; i++ {   // ONE ciphertext at a time
#            live at once: numThreads power bases. Worker count is the only knob. GELU is elementwise
#            (no joint need of the 768, unlike MOAI-GPU's LayerNorm).
# expected   ~27 GB off 253,723,168 kB (~11%):
#              EvaluateBabyStep        34.2 GB over 64 workers        = 535 MB per worker
#              (cross-check: degree-255 Chebyshev BSGS basis ~32 ct x 2 x 10 x 65536 x 8 B = ~335 MB)
#              48 workers fewer:       48 x 535 MB                    = ~25 GB
#              per-worker evaluator scratch (a2 census, three rows)   =  ~2 GB
# not hit    result array (768 ct, next stage's input), ringqp.Ring.NewPoly (42,127.9 MB), headroom.
# history    earlier prediction <1% counted only labelled per-worker rows (~2.7 GB); retired
#            floor(T) ~ 124.6 + 1.15*T (~55 GB) not comparable.
# cost       declared paid (4x fewer workers). If the saving is <1%, this is an operating-point study,
#            not a row.
